.Sh DESCRIPTION
The library behind
.Xr pcompress 1 .
A
.Vt pc_ctx_t
holds the settings for one job; the job turns a stream into the
.Pa .pz
format or back.
Several contexts may run at the same time in different threads of one
process.
One context must be used by one thread at a time.
The library installs no signal handlers and never exits the process.
.Pp
.Fn pc_new
returns a context with default settings: zstd, level 6 (lz4: level 1),
a chunk size chosen from the level, one thread per core, the BLAKE256
chunk checksum, and no deduplication or encryption.
.Fn pc_free
releases it and wipes any stored password.
A context can run any number of jobs; each job starts from its settings,
not from what the previous job left behind.
.Ss Settings
Each setter returns 0, or \-1 with
.Dv PC_ERR_ARGS
recorded for
.Fn pc_last_error .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn pc_set_algorithm
One of
.Qq zstd ,
.Qq lz4 ,
.Qq lzma ,
.Qq lzmaMt ,
.Qq bzip2 ,
.Qq zlib ,
.Qq ppmd ,
.Qq lzfx ,
.Qq libbsc ,
.Qq adapt ,
.Qq adapt2
or
.Qq none .
The name is not copied; pass a string that outlives the context.
.It Fn pc_set_level
1 (fastest) to 14 (smallest output).
.It Fn pc_set_chunk_size
The unit of parallelism, in bytes; 0 picks one from the level.
Deduplication needs at least 1 MB.
.It Fn pc_set_threads
0 means one per core.
.It Fn pc_set_checksum
.Qq BLAKE256 ,
.Qq BLAKE512 ,
.Qq SHA256 ,
.Qq SHA512 ,
.Qq KECCAK256 ,
.Qq KECCAK512 ,
.Qq SKEIN256 ,
.Qq SKEIN512
or
.Qq CRC64 .
.It Fn pc_set_dedupe
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_NONE ,
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_RABIN
(content-defined blocks within a chunk),
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_FIXED
(fixed blocks) or
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_GLOBAL .
Global deduplication needs a seekable output and is refused by
.Fn pc_compress_fd .
.It Fn pc_set_encryption
.Qq AES
or
.Qq SALSA20
in CTR mode with an HMAC per chunk;
.Dv NULL
turns encryption off.
.Fa keylen
is 16 or 32 bytes, 0 for 32.
.It Fn pc_set_password
The password, used by the next job; all of it feeds the key derivation
(scrypt).
It is copied; the caller's buffer is not changed.
(The
.Xr pcompress 1
prompt and
.Fl w
file read at most 16 bytes, so a longer password set here must also be
given here to decrypt.)
.El
.Ss Jobs
.Fn pc_compress_fd
reads
.Fa in_fd
to end of file and writes a
.Pa .pz
stream to
.Fa out_fd .
.Fn pc_decompress_fd
does the reverse and checks every chunk's checksum, or its HMAC if the
stream is encrypted.
Neither function closes its descriptors.
Both return 0 on success and \-1 on failure.
The output of a failed job is incomplete and must not be used.
.Pp
Streams written by these functions are the same as
.Ql pcompress -p
output, and either side can read the other's.
Archives
.Pq Fl a
and files written with global deduplication need
.Xr pcompress 1
or the command-line style API.
.Ss Diagnostics
Messages from all threads working for a context go to stderr, or to
.Fa fn
after
.Fn pc_set_log .
.Fa fn
is called as
.Fn fn arg level msg ,
never at the same time for one context.
.Fa level
is the highest level delivered:
.Dv PC_LOG_ERR ,
.Dv PC_LOG_WARN ,
.Dv PC_LOG_INFO
or
.Dv PC_LOG_VERBOSE ;
.Dv PC_LOG_NONE
delivers nothing, and \-1 uses the process default.
.Pp
.Fn pc_last_error
returns the first line of the first error in the last call on
.Fa ctx ,
or the empty string.
.Fn pc_last_error_code
returns its class:
.Bl -tag -width PC_ERR_INTERNAL -compact
.It Dv PC_OK
no error
.It Dv PC_ERR_ARGS
invalid setting or argument
.It Dv PC_ERR_IO
a read, write, open or stat failed
.It Dv PC_ERR_NOMEM
out of memory
.It Dv PC_ERR_FORMAT
not a
.Pa .pz
stream, or a newer format version
.It Dv PC_ERR_CORRUPT
damaged data
.It Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY
a checksum, CRC or HMAC did not match, or the password is wrong
.It Dv PC_ERR_CRYPTO
key setup failed or no password was given
.It Dv PC_ERR_INTERNAL
anything else
.El
.Fn pc_strerror
names a class.
.Pp
.Fn pc_cleanup_tempfiles
removes the temporary files of all running jobs.
It is async-signal-safe, for use in a program's own signal handler
before it exits.
.Ss Chunks
Building blocks for a backup tool that stores deduplicated, encrypted
objects:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn pc_chunker_new
A content-defined chunker: boundaries come from a rolling hash of the
data, so equal data gives equal chunks wherever it occurs, and an edit
changes only the chunks around it.
.Fa avg
(rounded down to a power of two),
.Fa min
and
.Fa max
are in bytes; 0 picks 64 KB, avg/4 and avg*4.
.It Fn pc_chunker_feed
Takes any amount of input and appends the length of each chunk that ends
in it to
.Fa lens ,
returning how many.
The result does not depend on how the input is split across calls.
Size
.Fa lens
as len / min + 1.
.Fn pc_chunker_finish
returns the last chunk.
.It Fn pc_chunk_id
BLAKE2b-256 of a chunk, keyed when
.Fa key
is given, so that object names do not reveal content hashes.
.It Fn pc_set_key
A raw 32-byte key for sealed objects.
.It Fn pc_chunk_seal
Compresses one chunk with the context's algorithm and level, stores it
raw if it does not shrink, and with a key encrypts it (XSalsa20, random
nonce) and authenticates header and payload (keyed BLAKE2b-256).
Without a key the object has an unkeyed BLAKE2b-256 checksum.
The output buffer needs
.Fn pc_chunk_seal_bound len
bytes.
.It Fn pc_chunk_open
Checks the tag before decrypting or decompressing, then restores the
chunk.
A changed or truncated object fails with
.Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY
or
.Dv PC_ERR_CORRUPT ,
a wrong key with
.Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY ,
a missing key with
.Dv PC_ERR_CRYPTO .
.Fn pc_chunk_open_size
reads the plaintext size from the header (unverified) to size the buffer.
.It Fn pc_keyfile_create
Makes a random 256-bit key and writes it, wrapped with a password of any
length, to
.Fn pc_keyfile_size
bytes at
.Fa out
(scrypt N=2^17 r=8 p=1, then XSalsa20 and a BLAKE2b-256 tag).
.Fn pc_keyfile_open
recovers the key; a wrong password or any change to the file fails.
The KDF and its parameters are stored in the file, and
.Fn pc_keyfile_open
refuses parameters beyond 512 MB or unreasonable work.
.El
.Pp
The object format is described in
.Pa DESIGN.md .
.Pa examples/pzstore.c
is a small deduplicating store built on these calls.
.Ss Command-line style API
.Fn create_pc_context ,
.Fn init_pc_context "ctx" "argc" "argv" ,
.Fn start_pcompress
and
.Fn destroy_pc_context
run a job described by
.Xr pcompress 1
arguments, with file names, archives and every option.
.Fn init_pc_context
returns 0, 1 for an invalid argument, 2 for a usage error, 3 for
.Fl h
and 4 for
.Fl V .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compress standard input to standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pcompress/pcompress.h>

int
main(void)
{
	pc_ctx_t *c = pc_new();
	int rv;

	pc_set_algorithm(c, "zstd");
	pc_set_level(c, 9);
	pc_set_log(c, NULL, NULL, PC_LOG_NONE);
	if ((rv = pc_compress_fd(c, 0, 1)) != 0)
		fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", pc_last_error(c));
	pc_free(c);
	return (rv != 0);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Build with
.Dl cc $(pkg-config --cflags libpcompress) x.c $(pkg-config --libs libpcompress)
.Pp
.Pa examples/pzcat.c
in the source tree is a complete filter;
.Pa examples/pzstore.c
a deduplicating, encrypted chunk store.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pcompress 1
.Sh HISTORY
The descriptor API, per-context logging and error classes appeared in
pcompress 4.1.
The command-line style API dates from 2.x.
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the symbols declared in
.In pcompress/pcompress.h
are exported.
The bundled zstd, lz4, libbsc and libarchive are internal, so a program
may link its own copies.
.Pp
The adaptive-mode counters printed by
.Fl C
are shared by all contexts in a process.
